+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/coastal-insurance/#article","headline":"Myrtle Beach Home Insurance: What It Costs, 6 Ways to Cut It","description":"Coastal home insurance on the Grand Strand: homeowners, wind, and flood policies explained, what each costs, why rental use raises premiums, and the programs that cut the bill.","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/coastal-insurance/","author":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"reviewedBy":{"@type":"Person","name":"Timothy Nash","alternateName":"Tim Nash","jobTitle":"Broker-in-Charge"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"datePublished":"2026-07-19","dateModified":"2026-10-27","inLanguage":"en-US","image":"https://chapter3realty.com/og-image.jpg","mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/coastal-insurance/#webpage"},"about":{"@type":"Thing","name":"Coastal home insurance in South Carolina"},"keywords":"Myrtle Beach home insurance cost, coastal insurance South Carolina, flood insurance Horry County, hurricane deductible, SC wind pool"}</script>
 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/coastal-insurance/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How much is homeowners insurance in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Local single-family policies commonly cost about $1,500 to $3,500 a year in the standard market, and comparison sites quoting a standardized $300,000 house put Myrtle Beach between about $4,500 and $5,300, among the highest in South Carolina. The honest answer is a range: age of roof, distance to the ocean, deductibles, and rental use swing the number by thousands. The spread between quotes on the same house is exactly why we shop every policy across multiple agents."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need flood insurance in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your lender requires it only if the home sits in a FEMA high-risk zone (AE or VE). But no homeowners policy covers flood, and FEMA reports that more than 1 in 4 flood claims come from outside high-risk zones. Horry County properties get a 25 percent discount on federal flood insurance through the county's rating, and lower-risk homes often cost a few hundred dollars a year to cover. We pull the flood zone on every property before you offer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the South Carolina wind pool, and will my policy exclude wind?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Inside the state wind pool's territory, insurers are allowed to exclude wind and hail from a homeowners policy. Around Myrtle Beach that territory reaches inland to Carolina Bays Parkway and River Oaks Drive. Farther south it reaches the Intracoastal Waterway. When that happens, you buy a separate wind policy, sometimes from the state's insurer of last resort, the South Carolina Wind and Hail Underwriting Association. About 1 in 6 coastal policies are written without wind. It is not a dealbreaker, it is a second premium to budget for, and we flag it before you offer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a hurricane deductible?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Coastal policies carry a percentage deductible for named storms instead of a flat dollar amount, usually 1 to 5 percent of the dwelling coverage. On a $400,000 house, a 2 percent hurricane deductible means the first $8,000 of storm damage is yours; at 5 percent it is $20,000. Know the percentage before you buy, and consider South Carolina's Catastrophe Savings Account, which lets you set aside that deductible free of state income tax."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does renting my house out raise my insurance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and hiding it is worse. A long-term rental needs a landlord policy, typically 15 to 25 percent more than a homeowner policy. Short-term rental use needs coverage written for it. Federal flood insurance also charges non-primary homes more: a $250 annual surcharge instead of $25, and premiums allowed to rise 25 percent a year instead of 18. If your insurer discovers rental use on its own, expect a repriced policy or a denied claim. Tell them, then shop the new premium hard."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How can I lower my coastal insurance premium?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The biggest levers are a fortified roof, wind mitigation credits insurers must disclose by law, and raising deductibles you can cover. Also compare the on-site quote against multiple carriers through an independent agent, and for flood, document your elevation. South Carolina's SC Safe Home program grants up to $7,500 toward a fortified roof, and Horry County received more of those grants in 2025 than any other county. Grantees of the roof program report premium cuts up to 24 percent."}}]}</script>
