the zone."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does a seller have to disclose past flooding in South Carolina?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "In most home sales, South Carolina sellers must complete a Residential Property Condition Disclosure Statement and disclose known material issues, including known water intrusion and flooding. A seller who knowingly hid past flooding may leave the buyer with legal recourse."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How can I find out if a house has flooded before?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Check the seller’s disclosure, a CLUE report of past insurance claims, physical clues like foundation staining or mismatched repairs, the drainage and history of the surrounding lots, and repair permits. A thorough agent digs all of this up before you offer."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What if the seller hid that the house flooded?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "If a seller knew and did not disclose it, you may have recourse under South Carolina disclosure law. Talk to a real estate attorney, but start by documenting the home’s real history, which is exactly what our building study produces."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "RealEstateAgent", "@id": "https://chapter3realty.com/#org", "name": "Chapter3 Realty", "legalName": "Chapter3 Realty Corp", "employee": [{"@type": "Person", "name": "Devin Day", "jobTitle": "Operations Officer", "image": "https://chapter3realty.com/team/devin.webp", "url": "https://chapter3realty.com/about/"}, {"@type": "Person", "name": "Timothy Nash", "alternateName": "Tim Nash", "jobTitle": "Broker-in-Charge", "image": "https://chapter3realty.com/team/timmy.webp", "url": "https://chapter3realty.com/about/"}, {"@type": "Person", "name": "Abdulla Hijazi", "jobTitle": "Chief Marketing Officer", "image": "https://chapter3realty.com/team/abdulla.webp", "url": "https://chapter3realty.com/about/"}], "founder": 