 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/invest/condotel-financing/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you get a mortgage on a condotel in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Most banks, credit unions, and franchise lenders will decline the building, but condotels close every week here through DSCR loans, portfolio loans, and other non-agency programs. Expect a larger down payment than a standard condo asks, and confirm the building with a lender before you write an offer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between a condotel and a non-warrantable condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A condotel is a condo building run like a hotel: front desk, nightly bookings, and units marketed as lodging. Non-warrantable is the broader lending label for any condo project that fails those rules, which includes condotels plus buildings with active litigation, low reserves, too much commercial space, or one owner holding too many units. Every condotel is non-warrantable, but not every non-warrantable building is a condotel."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What down payment does a condotel loan require?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Plan on a substantially larger down payment than a standard condo loan for most condotel and non-warrantable programs, and putting still more down buys stronger pricing. Exact requirements depend on the lender, the building, and whether the unit is a second home or an investment."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I use an FHA or VA loan to buy a condotel?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. FHA and VA loans require the condo project to be on their approved lists, and condotel buildings do not qualify. If you want FHA or VA financing on the Grand Strand, you will be shopping standard condo buildings or single-family homes instead."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do condotel loans cost more than regular condo loans?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Often, and more than they should. Because so few lenders offer the product, some that do treat it as a premium service and pad the fees, which hurts your cash flow from day one. Rates are higher than agency condo loans because the loan cannot be sold to Fannie or Freddie, but the lender fees do not have to be inflated. BrickWood Mortgage charges the same lender fees on a condotel file as on any other loan it originates."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find out if a building is warrantable before I offer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Ask a lender to review the project before you write the offer, not after. We keep current information on which Grand Strand buildings close cleanly and which ones need a specialty loan. Send us the building name and we will tell you what financing it takes."}}]}</script>
